Keep the IDF positive when a term statistic runs ahead of the document count

Applies to vchord_bm25 0.3.0 (upstream tag 0.3.0, commit c610b7ab), after 0001
to 0004.

The IDF (weight.rs) is ln((N + 1) / (n + 0.5)), where N is the number of live
documents and n the number of them that hold the term. It is positive while n
does not exceed N. A bulk delete takes its documents off N at once and off each
term's n only in the recount that follows (0004), so a VACUUM stopped between
the two leaves n above N for every term the deleted documents held, until the
next VACUUM finishes the recount. For a term held by more documents than now
remain, the IDF is then negative, every document that matches only that term
scores at or below 0, and the top-k scan, whose threshold starts at 0, keeps
none of them: a search for that term alone finds nothing.

- n is held at N, so the IDF is at least ln(1 + 0.5 / (N + 0.5)), which is
  positive.
- The ratio is taken in f64. In f32, (N + 1) / (N + 0.5) rounds to exactly 1
  once N passes about 8.4 million, so a term in every document already scored
  0 in a settled index of that size.

The seal path computes each block's summary with the same IDF (append.rs). A
seal while n ran ahead of N recorded summaries from a negative IDF, and a
bounded scan then skipped those blocks for good, even once the statistics were
recounted. This patch prevents that; an index an older build sealed in that
state keeps it until REINDEX.

Measured on 100,000 documents with a term in every one, 40,000 deleted, after a
VACUUM cancelled between the bulk delete and the cleanup: a search for that
term alone returned no rows, and 10 with this patch (bm25_limit 10). On a
settled index of 45,000 documents, 58 queries returned the same top 20 in the
same order with and without it, and no score in them moved by more than 1.4e-7
of itself. A term held by nearly every document moves more, by up to about
0.2% at 45,000 to 2.1 million documents: f32 rounded its ratio. The move is the
same for every document holding the term, so no order changes.

